{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00366 - MONDO_1040",
  "verbalization": "Doxylamine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Nasopharyngitis through a chain of indications that link it to Common Cold and subsequently to the condition. The evidence suggests that Doxylamine may be associated with Common Cold, which in turn is linked to Nasopharyngitis via indirect pathways. This relationship is supported by the presence of multiple overlapping indications, including Bromodiphenhydramine, Hydromorphone, and Guaiacol, all of which are associated with Nasopharyngitis. The shared indication of Common Cold across these pathways indicates a potential mechanistic link, possibly involving immune system activation or inflammatory responses. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Common Cold as a central mediator in the relationship between Doxylamine and Nasopharyngitis, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
